1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,660
Good morning everyone, and welcome to the Museum of Farming Life.

2
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:08,580
I understand it's your first visit here

3
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:12,800
so I'd like to give you some background information about the museum

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:17,100
and then explain a little about what you can see during your visit.

5
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:29,120
So, where we're standing at the moment is the entrance to a large building that was constructed in 1880 as the home of a local businessman,

6
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:32,480
Alfred Palmer, of the Palmer biscuit factory.

7
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:39,420
It was later sold and became a hall of residence for students in 1911

8
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:43,300
and a museum in 1951.

9
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:50,060
In 2005, a modern extension was built to accommodate the museum's collections.

10
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:54,200
The museum's owned by the university,

11
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,240
and apart from two rooms that are our offices

12
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,780
the university uses the main part of the building.

13
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,840
You may see students going into the building for lessons,

14
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:09,140
but it's not open to museum visitors, I'm afraid.

15
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:15,060
It's a shame because the interior architectural features are outstanding,

16
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,220
especially the room that used to be the library.

17
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:24,220
Luckily, we've managed to keep entry to the museum free.

18
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:27,620
This includes access to all the galleries

19
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:31,600
outdoor areas and the rooms for special exhibitions.

20
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:36,440
We run activities for children and students

21
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:40,480
such as the museum club, for which there's no charge.

22
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:45,140
We do have a donation box just over there

23
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:49,220
so feel free to give whatever amount you consider appropriate.

24
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:55,300
We do have a cloakroom, if you'd like to leave your coats and bags somewhere.

25
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:01,160
Unlike other museums, photography is allowed here

26
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:04,220
so you might like to keep your cameras with you.

27
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:09,560
You might be more comfortable not carrying around heavy rucksacks,

28
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:14,720
though keep your coats and jackets on as it's quite cold in the museum garden today.

29
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:19,400
I'd like to tell you about the different areas of the museum.

30
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:26,780
Just inside, and outside the main gallery, we have an area called Four Seasons.

31
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:31,820
Here you can watch a four-minute animation of a woodland scene.

32
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:39,880
It was designed especially for the museum by a group of young people on a film studies course, and it's beautiful.

33
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:43,820
Children absolutely love it, but then, so do adults.

34
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:47,760
The main gallery's called Town and Country.

35
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:53,980
It includes a photographic collection of prizewinning sheep and shepherds.

36
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:59,980
Leaving Town and Country, you enter Farmhouse Kitchen, which is... well, self-explanatory.

37
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:06,240
Here we have the oldest collection of equipment for making butter

38
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:08,020
and cheese in the country.

39
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:14,640
And this morning, a specialist cheesemaker will be giving demonstrations of how it's produced.

40
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:16,700
You may even get to try some.

41
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:21,300
After that, you can go in two directions.

42
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:26,640
To the right is a staircase that takes you up to a landing from

43
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,920
where you can look down on the galleries.

44
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:33,440
To the left is a room called A Year on the Farm.

45
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:38,800
There's lots of seating here as sometimes we use the room for school visits,

46
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:41,600
so it's a good place to stop for a rest.

47
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:43,870
If you're feeling competitive,

48
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:47,260
you can take our memory test in

49
00:03:47,260 --> 00:03:50,600
which you answer questions about things you've seen in the museum.

50
00:03:51,860 --> 00:03:53,940
The next area's called Wagon Walk

51
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:59,180
This contains farm carts from nearly every part of the country.

52
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:04,460
It's surprising how much regional variation there was.

53
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:08,860
Beside the carts are display boards with information about each one.

54
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,540
The carts are old and fragile,

55
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:16,020
so we ask you to keep your children close to you

56
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:18,380
and ensure they don't climb on the carts.

57
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:26,120
From Wagon Walk, you can either make your way back to reception or go out into the garden -

58
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:29,720
or even go back to take another look in the galleries.

59
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:34,560
In the far corner of the garden is Bees are Magic,

60
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:38,880
but we're redeveloping this area so you can't visit that at the moment.

61
00:04:39,560 --> 00:04:42,100
You can still buy our honey in the shop, though.

62
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:48,680
Finally, there's The Pond, which contains all kinds of interesting wildlife.

63
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:54,380
There are baby ducks that are only a few days old, as well as tiny frogs.

64
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:58,600
The Pond isn't deep and there's a fence around it

65
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:00,920
so it's perfectly safe for children.
